25,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It seems the catalogue-style projects have a lasting legacy, whereas the mechanism-focused ones often leave questions unanswered.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
26,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"We have a shortage of phenotyping technicians, but plenty of interest in modelling and code. However, senior analysts are currently abroad.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
27,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The animal facilities are in good shape, but the spectroscopy suite had a significant lapse in service last year.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
28,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Computing is currently free, but animal-unit costs have spiked. Also, shipping costs are rising while per-diems remain frozen.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
29,2,1,talk,Midori,,"Field sites have been hit hard by disasters, though indoor facilities are stable. Fortunately, the repository turnaround is now very fast.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
30,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Ryuji, does the lack of phenotyping staff make you wary of projects relying on heavy manual data collection?","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"Yes, it does. Projects requiring specialized technical hands for biological sampling are at higher risk than those based on simulation.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the rise in animal-unit costs might make some proposals financially precarious if they aren't carefully budgeted.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. And if they rely on the spectroscopy suite Victoria mentioned, they might face unexpected delays.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, if catalogue projects are more reliable, perhaps we should favor the more descriptive proposals over the theoretical ones.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I agree, Midori. Stability and utility often outweigh the allure of a high-risk mechanism study.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"I am leaning toward computation-heavy work, given the current staffing trends and the free computing agreement Benjamin noted.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"But we must consider the hardware. If the spectroscopy suite is unstable, simulation-only or animal-based work is safer.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,It seems we are converging on a preference for projects that avoid the current bottlenecks in staffing and field stability.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I am concerned about any project relying on field sites, given the recent flooding and fires.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
